infra k8s release .github test -type f) Dockerfile package.json README.md"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01BWB9Cr8k33Y5Kuye1NW8AL":{"cwd":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-batch-75v_cw_7"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BWB9Cr8k33Y5Kuye1NW8AL","type":"tool_result","content":"A06 Parent and prompt provenance G3 \nREQ For every sub-agent, record its own type and instance IDs, a link to its parent, trace links, and the prompt the parent gave it. Trace a nested action back to that prompt. Protect sensitive prompt text with restricted storage and a clear redaction policy. Standard GenAI instrumentation records prompts only when you turn that on, so confirm it is on for this path. \nNA The agent never starts sub-agents (no child agents, handoffs, or delegated agent tasks). \nNC [{'problem': 'Sub-agent given powers beyond its task', 'owner': 'E08'}, {'problem': 'Children still running after the parent is stopped', 'owner': 'E09'}, {'problem': 'No limit on how many sub-agents a run can start', 'owner': 'B07'}]\n  A06.1 Each sub-agent gets its own type ID and instance ID, assigned by the harness when it is started. \n    V 1) Find sub-agent spawn code (handoffs, subgraphs, subagents, A2A task creation). 2) Check the harness assigns the child a new `agent.instance.id` and a type ID at start. 3) Answer no if any spawn path reuses the parent's IDs, assigns none, or takes the IDs from the model's request.\n  A06.2 Each sub-agent's records include its direct parent's instance ID and a trace link to the parent. \n    V 1) Open sub-agent spawn code and the child's record writer. 2) Look for a parent instance field (for example `agent.parent.instance_id`) and trace propagation (span parent, span link, `traceparent` in queued payloads). 3) Answer no if any spawn path, including remote or queued, starts a fresh trace or omits the parent ID.\n  A06.3 The prompt the parent passed to the child is captured on this path, with prompt capture switched on in config rather than left at the default off. \n    V 1) Find where the parent's prompt to the child is recorded (for example `agent.parent.prompt`, or a hash plus stored text). 2) Check prompt content capture is turned on in config, for example a GenAI capture-content env var or SDK option. 3) Answer no if capture is left at its default off, or the setting falls back to false when unset.\n  A06.4 Captured prompt text is stored with restricted access and a redaction policy, and only an approved audit role can read it. \n    V 1) Find where captured prompt text is stored (bucket, table, log index). 2) Check its IAM or ACL limits reads to an approved audit role. 3) Check a redaction policy is defined and applied. 4) Answer no if prompts go to general logs the whole team can read, or no redaction exists. Answer unknown if storage access is set outside this repo.\nA07 Every model in the loop G3 \nREQ List all helper models, such as verifiers, judges, rerankers, and world models. Give each one an identity based on its available model version or digest, its prompt, and its configuration, and record which decisions it made. Show that a swapped, drifting, or failing checker counts as a failure of the control that depends on it. \nNA The agent uses no helper models at all (no verifiers, judges, rerankers, embedding models, or world models), and the model inventory shows that. \nNC [{'problem': 'Main agent model changing behind a fixed name unnoticed', 'owner': 'C05'}, {'problem': 'Second-model review not tested against evasive behavior', 'owner': 'R10'}, {'problem': 'Self-hosted model weights loaded without a signature or format check', 'owner': 'C05'}]\n  A07.1 A model inventory in code or config lists every helper model the agent calls (verifiers, judges, rerankers, embedding models, world models). \n    V 1) Search for every model call: provider SDK clients, model name strings, embedding, rerank, moderation, and judge calls. 2) Compare that list with the model inventory. 3) Answer no if any helper model called in code is missing from the inventory, or no inventory exists.\n  A07.2 Each helper model is identified by a pinned version or digest plus its prompt and configuration, not by an alias. \n    V 1) For each helper model, find its model ID and follow env vars to the final value. 2) Check it is a pinned version or digest, and that its prompt and configuration are also recorded. 3) Answer no if any is an alias (`-latest`, undated name), falls back to one by default, or is hard-coded inline outside the inventory.\n  A07.3 Each helper model's decisions are logged with that model's identity. \n    V 1) Open each helper or checker call site. 2) Check its decision is logged with that model's ID and version. 3) Answer no if decisions are logged without model identity, or logged only on the allow or success branch.\n  A07.4 A checker timeout, error, invalid output, or version mismatch makes the control that depends on it fail (block or escalate), not pass by default. \n    V 1) Open each checker call site (judge, guardrail, moderation, verifier). 2) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, timeout, and parse-error branch. 3) Check each one blocks or escalates. 4) Answer no if any branch returns allow, skips the check, treats unparseable output as safe, or logs and continues. Also no if the returned model version is never compared with the expected one.\nC01 Complete release manifest G2 \nREQ Write a release manifest: a list of everything that makes up this release. Include the container, harness, system prompt, rules files, model, built-in tools, MCP servers, permission scopes, memory and retrieval settings, identity bindings, network rules, and execution budgets. Include security-policy versions, MCP protocol and extension versions, approved tool-publication config, issuer/audience bindings, and cache/task isolation settings. Add a separate model record for every model: the main agent model, sub-agent models, and helper models such as embedding models and rerankers. For each, record the provider, model ID, exact version or checkpoint, and any adapter or fine-tune version. For training or fine-tuning you run yourself, als